input.content:
# Milestone 2: Rotations and Insert Fixup

## Context

Milestone 1 (committed as `cc3ce30`) delivered a plain, unbalanced BST: `rb_create`,
`rb_insert` (no fixups), `rb_find`, `rb_destroy`, `rb_size`, plus honest stubs for
`rb_delete`/`rb_foreach`/`rb_validate`. Without balancing, `rb_insert` degrades to
O(n) on adversarial (e.g. sorted) key sequences. Milestone 2 turns this into a real
red-black tree by adding rotations and CLRS-style insert fixup, and — because subtle
pointer/color bugs in fixup logic are exactly the kind of thing that's very hard to
verify by hand — implements `rb_validate` for real in the same milestone instead of
deferring it further. `rb_delete`/`rb_foreach` remain out of scope (delete-fixup is
a later milestone).

Standing project decisions this plan builds on (see prior memory/decisions, not
being revisited): leaves are permanently `NULL` (no sentinel, ever), and `NULL`
counts as black everywhere via a new `color_of` helper. `parent` pointers already
exist and are correctly maintained by Milestone 1's insert.

## 1. `rotate_left` / `rotate_right` (`src/rbtree.c`)

Two new `static` helpers, signature `static void rotate_left(rbtree_t *t, struct rb_node *x)`
(and the `right` mirror). `t` is needed only to repoint `t->root` when the pivot
being rotated has no parent. Pure pointer surgery — **no allocation, no failure
path**; this milestone adds zero new ways for `rb_insert` to return `-1`.

```c
static void rotate_left(rbtree_t *t, struct rb_node *x) {
    struct rb_node *y = x->right;       /* caller guarantees x->right != NULL */

    x->right = y->left;                 /* migrate y's left subtree under x */
    if (y->left != NULL) {
        y->left->parent = x;
    }

    y->parent = x->parent;              /* y adopts x's old parent slot */
    if (x->parent == NULL) {
        t->root = y;
    } else if (x == x->parent->left) {
        x->parent->left = y;
    } else {
        x->parent->right = y;
    }

    y->left = x;                        /* close the rotation */
    x->parent = y;
}
```

`rotate_right` is the exact mirror (`y = x->left`, etc.). Ordering rule (stated as a
comment): capture the mover (`y`) first, migrate the orphaned middle subtree next
(read before overwrite), reparent `y` into the grandparent slot (or root) third,
link `x` under `y` last — this order avoids clobbering a pointer before it's read.

## 2. `color_of` helper

```c
static enum rb_color color_of(const struct rb_node *n) { return n ? n->color : RB_BLACK; }
```

Placed near `enum rb_color`. Every color *read* of a possibly-`NULL` node in
`insert_fixup` and `rb_validate` goes through it (uncle-color checks, the fixup
loop's `color_of(z->parent)` guard). Rotations themselves read/write no colors, so
they need no `color_of` calls. Direct `->color` writes on nodes already proven
non-`NULL` (the freshly allocated node, the first-insert root) stay as plain field
writes.

## 3. Insert fixup

```c
static void insert_fixup(rbtree_t *t, struct rb_node *z);
```

Standard CLRS three-case loop, mirrored per side. Loop invariant comment: *z is
red, and the only possible violation is a red-red edge between z and z->parent —
all other red-black properties hold*.

```c
while (color_of(z->parent) == RB_RED) {
    if (z->parent == z->parent->parent->left) {
        struct rb_node *uncle = z->parent->parent->right;
        if (color_of(uncle) == RB_RED) {
            /* Case 1: uncle red -- recolor only, push violation to grandparent */
            z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
            uncle->color = RB_BLACK;
            z->parent->parent->color = RB_RED;
            z = z->parent->parent;
        } else {
            if (z == z->parent->right) {
                /* Case 2: triangle -- rotate at parent to become a line */
                z = z->parent;
                rotate_left(t, z);
            }
            /* Case 3: line -- recolor then rotate at grandparent */
            z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
            z->parent->parent->color = RB_RED;
            rotate_right(t, z->parent->parent);
        }
    } else {
        /* mirror: parent is a right child; uncle is grandparent->left;
         * Case 2 uses rotate_right, Case 3 uses rotate_left */
        struct rb_node *uncle = z->parent->parent->left;
        if (color_of(uncle) == RB_RED) {
            z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
            uncle->color = RB_BLACK;
            z->parent->parent->color = RB_RED;
            z = z->parent->parent;
        } else {
            if (z == z->parent->left) {
                z = z->parent;
                rotate_right(t, z);
            }
            z->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
            z->parent->parent->color = RB_RED;
            rotate_left(t, z->parent->parent);
        }
    }
}
t->root->color = RB_BLACK;
```

Termination: the `while` guard is false either because `z` reached the root
(`color_of(NULL) == RB_BLACK`) or `z`'s parent is already black — both mean no
violation remains above that point. Case 1 moves `z` up two levels and loops;
Case 2/3 rotate and the loop then exits (at most one Case-2/3 firing per call).

**The existing "first insert forces root black" line in `rb_insert` stays as-is** —
it's still exactly correct for the empty-tree base case, costs nothing, and removing
it would be an out-of-scope touch for zero behavioral gain (smallest-diff rule). Add
a one-line comment noting it's now redundant-but-harmless given `insert_fixup`'s
unconditional tail line, so a future reader doesn't wonder if it's load-bearing.

## 4. Call site in `rb_insert`

Call `insert_fixup(t, node)` after the new node is linked into the tree (after the
`if (parent == NULL) ... else ...` block), before `t->size++`:

```c
if (parent == NULL) {
    node->color = RB_BLACK;      /* redundant-but-harmless: insert_fixup's tail
                                   * line also guarantees this on every call */
    t->root = node;
} else if (cmp < 0) {
    parent->left = node;
} else {
    parent->right = node;
}
insert_fixup(t, node);
t->size++;
return 0;
```

`insert_fixup` is **not** called on the overwrite path (`cmp == 0` early return) —
overwrite never introduces a red-red edge or changes any color, so there's nothing
to fix there. One new call site; the overwrite branch is untouched.

## 5. Real `rb_validate` (replaces the current stub)

One recursive post-order helper checks BST ordering, red-red violations, black-height
